<section class="soc-sec soc-howto"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">How to read the ladders</h2><p>Each ladder runs from the top of its society to the bottom, by rank in law and honour rather than by wealth alone: a rich Roman freedman still stood below the freeborn. Every rank has a share of the whole population, children included, where scholars have estimated one, and says so where nobody has counted. To compare societies, every rank is also placed in one of eight broad kinds: ruler, nobility and officials, religious and learned, soldiers, merchants and craftsmen, free commoners, serfs and dependants, outcast groups, enslaved.</p></section>
